e('Before virtualisation, data centres ran one application per server for safety, and typical servers sat at 5–15% CPU. Packing ten VMs onto one host cuts hardware, power and space.','Separate lightly loaded physical servers can share one host as VMs, potentially reducing hardware, power and space when capacity and isolation requirements permit.','Remove unsupported historical utilisation range and guaranteed ten-to-one consolidation saving.')
e('A crash, a kernel panic or a compromise in one guest stays in that guest.','A guest kernel panic is normally confined to that VM. Hypervisor/device-model vulnerabilities, shared resources and host failures can still affect other guests.','Isolation is a boundary, not a guarantee against escape or shared-host failure.')
e('A VM is just state: a virtual disk file plus memory and CPU registers. You can snapshot it, copy it, or move it to another host while it runs.','A VM has disk, memory, CPU and virtual-device state. Supported configurations can snapshot or migrate that state; external services, passthrough devices and destination compatibility can constrain this.','Include device/external state and avoid arbitrary migration guarantee.')
e('Every EC2 instance, Azure VM and Google Compute Engine instance is a guest on someone else\'s hypervisor. Selling slices of a machine is only possible because of this.','Cloud virtual-machine offerings use hypervisors to share hardware. Bare-metal offerings such as EC2 metal instances are an exception; cloud provisioning itself does not require a guest hypervisor.','EC2 includes bare-metal instances; cloud provisioning is not synonymous with VM execution.',['https://aws.amazon.com/documentation-overview/ec2/'])
e('The classic split (from Robert Goldberg\'s 1973 thesis) is about what the hypervisor runs on.','The classic split is about what the hypervisor runs on.','Remove unsourced thesis date attribution.')
e('| Overhead | Lower | Higher |','| Overhead | Implementation/workload dependent | Implementation/workload dependent |','Type label alone does not establish performance ordering.')
e('guests are ordinary Linux processes, yet they run on hardware with the kernel as the VMM. Most people call it type 1.','a userspace VMM commonly represents each VM as a process with vCPU threads, while KVM provides kernel virtualisation support. It is commonly classified as type 1.','Guest OS not literally an ordinary process; distinguish userspace VMM representation.',['https://docs.kernel.org/virt/kvm/api.html'])
