e('Their theorem: an architecture can be virtualised with trap-and-emulate if **every sensitive instruction is also privileged**.','Their theorem gives a **sufficient condition**, within its formal machine model: a VMM can be constructed if every sensitive instruction is privileged.','Do not convert sufficient condition into universal necessary-and-sufficient rule.',[pg])
e('In user mode it **silently ignores** that bit, with no trap.','In the conventional protected-mode case with CPL greater than IOPL (excluding virtual-8086 complications), it leaves IF unchanged without trapping merely because of that attempted change.','State privilege/mode preconditions; not all user-mode POPF variants share this behavior.',[a])
e('Guest **user** code ran directly, since user-mode instructions behave the same in a VM. Only the kernel needed translation.','Guest **user** code normally ran directly at its expected privilege level, with the VMM controlling memory and handling traps as needed. This avoids translating the application instruction stream; it does not imply every observable machine detail or timing is identical.', 'User-mode direct execution still relies on memory virtualization and trap handling.',[a])
e('Performance was excellent, close to native. The cost was that you needed the source code of the guest kernel. Linux was ported; Windows could not be.','The 2003 Xen paper reported low overhead for its evaluated workloads. Kernel adaptation was required; its Windows XP port was incomplete but already ran some user-space programs. Unmodified Windows was not supported by that PV interface.','Original paper explicitly reports Windows XP port, contrary to categorical impossibility.',[x])
e('| Interpretation | None | 10–100x slower |','| Interpretation | None | Per-instruction software work |','Remove unverified fixed slowdown.')
e('| Paravirtualisation | Kernel ported | Guest source needed |','| Paravirtualisation | Kernel adapted | Porting and maintenance |','Essential condition is guest adaptation, not availability of open source.',[x])
e('CPU-bound code in a modern VM runs within a few per cent of native.','CPU-bound code can approach native speed, but there is no universal overhead percentage.','Remove unsupported workload-independent performance promise.')
e('That is the source of consolidation savings, and of "noisy neighbour" problems and *steal time* (time your vCPU was ready but the host ran someone else).','Overcommit can improve utilisation but is not required for consolidation. Contention can produce noisy-neighbour effects and steal time: reported periods when a vCPU was runnable but not scheduled.','Consolidation need not overcommit; steal does not identify another guest as unique cause.',[k])
e('Popek–Goldberg: trap-and-emulate works only if every sensitive instruction is privileged. Classic x86 broke this (`POPF`, `SGDT`).','Popek–Goldberg gives a sufficient virtualisability condition in its model. Classic x86 had sensitive instructions that did not reliably trap (`POPF`, `SGDT`), obstructing simple trap-and-emulate.','Correct necessary-condition overstatement.',[pg,a])
e('## Further reading','> [!note] Evidence gaps\n> No reproducible benchmark supports a universal VM overhead, interpretation slowdown or historical server-utilisation percentage here, so those figures are omitted. Snapshot/migration support and isolation depend on the actual platform and configuration.\n\n## Further reading','Make unsupported performance evidence omissions visible.')
