z(3,'workedExample','Consolidation can share spare capacity. Separate guest kernels normally contain a guest panic. Encapsulated state supports snapshots and migration where devices and destination are compatible. None of these mechanisms guarantees a CPU speedup.','Replace anywhere/never-faster absolutes.')
z(4,'prompt','On classic 32-bit x86 protected mode, assume valid POPFD stack access, CPL greater than IOPL and no virtual-8086 mode. A deprivileged guest kernel tries to set IF. What happens to that attempted IF change?','State exact exception-free context for ignored IF update.',[a])
z(4,'explanation','The attempted IF update under the stated conditions does not itself raise a fault. Other faults, such as an invalid stack access, are excluded here.','POPF can fault for other reasons.',[a],1)
z(4,'workedExample','With CPL above IOPL in the stated protected-mode case, POPFD leaves IF unchanged without trapping for that change. A simple VMM therefore misses the intended virtual interrupt-state update. If its virtual IF was disabled, it can remain disabled and delay guest progress. Binary translation can replace this operation with an explicit virtual-state update.','Avoid blanket changes-all-other-flags and unconditional hang.',[a])
z(7,'prompt','Why did VMware\'s classical software VMM normally execute guest user code directly while translating guest kernel code?','Normal implementation strategy rather than universal equivalence.',[a])
z(7,'explanation','User code can contain sensitive instructions. Direct execution works for the normal application path at its expected privilege level, together with memory virtualisation and trap handling.','SGDT disclosure means not every observable result identical.',[a],0)
